{
  "question": "Does Westpac's low FY25 impairment charge show that credit-risk normalisation is complete, or does the September 2025 provision stock still embed material caution? Reconcile the charge flow, the CAP/IAP movements, overlays and the probability-weighted/base/downside ECL scenarios.",
  "bank": "WBC",
  "periods": [
    "FY25"
  ],
  "answer": "## Answer\n\n**Both are true, but the second dominates.** The FY25 charge flow is genuinely normalised-to-benign \u2014 $424m, 5bps of average loans (FY24: 7bps), and only 4bps in 2H25. But the September 2025 provision stock still embeds material caution: reported probability-weighted ECL of $4,987m sits **$1.9bn above the 100% base case ECL of $3,040m**, the downside weight was lifted 5ppt over the year to 47.5%, and overlays were rebuilt +$59m to $238m. Normalisation is complete in the *flow*, not conceded in the *stock*.\n\n### Charge flow\nFY25 impairment charge $424m vs $537m FY24; 5bps vs 7bps (2H25 $174m, 4bps) [ev-1, ev-2, ev-26]. But the fall is not driven by better loss emergence: Westpac says it was \"mainly due to an increase in write-backs and recoveries partly offset by a higher CAP charge\" [ev-10]. Underlying loss realisation actually rose \u2014 CAP write-offs $561m vs $486m [ev-6]; net write-offs to average gross loans 6bps vs 5bps, i.e. *above* the 5bps charge [ev-3].\n\n### CAP/IAP movements\n- **IAPs** swung to a $34m net benefit from a $140m FY24 charge, on write-backs $195m (from $93m) and recoveries $247m (from $190m) [ev-4, ev-8, ev-9]. New IAPs were near-flat at $408m [ev-7]. The 2H25 IAP stock fall of $72m was a *single name write-off* in the trade sector, not credit improvement [ev-22].\n- **CAP charge rose** to $458m from $397m [ev-5]. \"Other changes in CAP\" benefit shrank each half: (147)/(76)/(27) $m \u2014 \"Improvement in economics, partly offset by overlays\" [ev-39, ev-38].\n- **Stock**: modelled CAP fell $168m to $4,201m; overlays +$59m to $238m; total CAP $4,439m; IAP $539m; total ECL $4,987m (\u20112%) [ev-13, ev-14, ev-15, ev-16, ev-17].\n\n### Overlays\nOverlays rose $108m in 2H25 alone [ev-25], for \"portfolio seasoning and geographical areas experiencing higher stress not included in modelled outcomes\" [ev-23] \u2014 new caution, offsetting releases of construction/mortgage/consumer-finance overlays. Still below Sep-23's $432m [ev-33].\n\n### Scenarios\nWeights shifted *away* from benign: upside 5.0%\u21922.5%, base 52.5%\u219250.0%, downside 42.5%\u219247.5% [ev-18, ev-19, ev-20]. Downside assumes \u20116% GDP, 11% unemployment, \u201127% house prices, \u201132% commercial property [ev-27, ev-28, ev-30] \u2014 trough/peak values, not forecasts [ev-36].\n\n### Counter-evidence (supports normalisation)\nMortgage 90+ 1.05%\u21920.70% [ev-12]; stressed exposures \u201117bps to 1.28% [ev-46]; Stage 2 CAP $2,390m\u2192$2,087m [ev-35].\n\n### Not settled\nCoverage ratios fell \u2014 provisions/gross loans 63\u219258bps, CAP/credit RWA 130\u2192125bps [ev-42, ev-43] \u2014 so caution is *reallocated*, not increased. Impaired exposures grew to $2,013m with cover down to 39.53% [ev-49, ev-50], and non-performing <90 days rose 7bps to 0.30% [ev-47]: early-stage formation is still building.",
